This one matters to me a great deal, because it's my first genuine honest
ascension, and I *think* it's also the first "Drunk" ascension to be posted.

As it says in my RECORD file,

No	Points	Name					Hp [max]
1      1093764  Daarin-D ascended to demigod-hood	131 [131]


No abuses at all, not even savefile duping or polypiling or my own special 
favorite, going into wizard mode and "salting" ghost levels. [*] I *did*, 
however, get an honest ghost pile from a Wilbur-the-Wizard who'd got hisself 
kilt a while back.  We'll get to that.

********************************************************************************
[*]  I believe I invented this one myself.  You salt ghost levels by wishing up 
a good set of stuff, everything near the stairs, going to another room, and
summoning a newt to kill you.  Because you dropped it before you got killed, it 
isn't technically a ghost pile and doesn't get cursed.  You do this at about 
level 3.  Oh, and you level-teleport there so your cat/dog won't be hanging
around to annoy your future self when you're putting on that +8 GDSM and so
on.  Anyway, I didn't do that on this one.
********************************************************************************

Daarin started out with a potion of holy water, a couple potions of water, two 
uncursed gain levels, two extra healing, and a blessed gain ability.  Daarin
immediately quaffed the gain ability, the gain levels, and the x-healing (in
that order, to maximize HP), and was ready to clean out level one.

At level three, he was still a third level Wino who hadn't killed much of 
anything when Steppin (his cat) brought him an amulet (not cursed.  Must be
okay.) and a "polished silver shield."  The latter proved to be exactly what I 
knew it must be, and helped right away with slaying a floating eye.  Nice when
those gaze weapons reflect, ainnit?

By level five, he'd found enough fountains to (d)ip all his useless potions into
water and then combined them and dipped them to make 8 potions of HW.  It was
at this point he ran into Wilbur's ghost -- right by the stairs when he went
down to L6.  Hit Wilbur's ghost once to get its attention, go up to L5, run
around a little to lose the ghost, down to 6, and -- voila!  GDSM, Helm of
Telepathy, Athame, Magic Marker, and a bunch of scrolls.  One genocide --
dip it into an HW and bye-bye monsters beginning with L.  Happy, happy, happy;
joy, joy, joy.  Bunches of fountains, several charges of the magic marker to 
make blessed enchanters, and two bags of tricks worn out of charges by an altar 
later, Daarin had:

	+7 Excalibur
	+8 Athame
	+5 GDSM
	+3 helm of TP
	+4 gauntlets of dexterity
	+5 Hawaiian shirt
	+3 speed boots
	+5 shield-o-reflection

... and all but the helm were rustproofed.  To make matters even worse, I 
finally got to ID that amulet and it was a life-saving!

By the time Daarin reached the castle level, he was L10 and had reverse-
genocided enough tengus to get that all-important teleport control.  Steppin,
by now a large cat, was still with him.

Unfortunately, there was no way to open the castle gate.  Okay, use levitation 
and go the long way around.  Fine.  Then fight back through to the front to let
the cat in, kicking down the drawbridge and then pushing a frost giant's boulder
into the moat to give the little bugger something to walk across on.

Get the wand of wishing.  Save it for now.

No special need to get anything from the store-rooms; a cloak would be nice but
the AC was already embarassing.  So stick a leash on Steppin and pop down one
of those trap doors to where we can confuse ourselves down to level 50 --

-- and would you believe Daarin had never managed to get fire resistance?  Thank
*god* for that amulet, which, as a special one-time-only bonus, popped Daarin
back up to the castle.  Wish up a blessed tin of red dragon meat.  Then another 
amulet of life-saving.  I already had a blessed scroll of charging (looove that 
magic marker!), so I saved the last original charge on the wand for time of
need.

Back down to the first level of Hell.  As long as we're here, let's find the 
stairs up to help us when we're running from the Wiz.

And -- DAMMIT! -- the #$%$#%^!(!!! cat (whom is running free until it's time to
level teleport) steps on a level teleporter.  I never saw him again.  I really
wanted to finish this game with the original pet!

Find the stairs.  Get confused.  Tport down to 50.

And lo and behold:  the Wiz is on 48.

The rest of the game is simple -- kill the Wiz and run, run, run.  Daarin had to
kill him something like thirty times before he got to the endgame.  The only
really tough moment was when he was fainting from hunger in Hell (and so 
couldn't pray) and the only thing around to kill was a Quantum Mechanic, which 
of course lost him the haste intrinsic.  Then, while still hungry, the Wiz 
showed up and -- showing his usual deft sense of timing -- went into "Oh no!
Double Trouble!" mode *and* summoned up a bunch of pets including a pair of
Titans.

The second life-saving got used up there, and the last "natural" charge of the
wand got burned to get a new one, but Daarin popped up a level killing all the 
pets and a pair of Wizzes.  Then the scroll of charging turned out to have been 
the subject of one of those "malignant auras" and the wand turned into a pumpkin
in front of his face.  But he never needed the last amulet, as it turned out,
and he was able to get satiated eating the Wizard's deceased pets and the Wizard
("You cannibal! You will regret this!" -- yeah, right; he already had the annoy
monster intrinsic as a result of a cursed spellbook, and Dionysus wasn't going
to do him a durn bit of good in Hell anyway, so he had plenty of time to earn 
his favor back.

Decided *not* to risk Vlad's castle while being pursued by the Wizard, which 
proved to be probably a good idea, because the Wiz showed up while Daarin was
on one of the tower levels.  Wizard, Vlad, and pets would probably have burned
that last life-saving amulet.

Endgame was trivial.  The roomful of rust monsters got incredibly frustrated
with all Daarin's rustproof shit.

*SIGH*  Now I've done it.  The Game That Ate My Brain can finally let go.

(Or maybe I can be the first to ascend in *all* NH++ character classes.  Yeah,
that's the ticket.  Next up:  Urnando the Undertaker. . .)


